@page "/docs/registry/search"
<PageTitle>Registry search - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Search</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">opt-in for servers</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl text-muted-foreground">Every search works against every registry. A static registry hands over its catalogue and the CLI filters locally; a registry with a server behind it can answer the search itself, and the CLI notices and trusts it. Neither side needs to be told which kind it is talking to.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Searching</h2>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/SearchCommands" />
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground"><code>-q</code> matches name, title and description, comma-separated alternatives. <code>-t</code> keeps only the listed item types, with or without their <code>registry:</code> prefix. <code>-l</code> and <code>--offset</code> page the result. Under <code>--json</code> the output carries a <code>pagination</code> object, so scripts read totals instead of counting.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The protocol</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">The search travels as query parameters on the catalogue request - the same URL, the same credentials, nothing new to route:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/SearchRequest" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50">
                    <BzTableHead>Parameter</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>q</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The query. How it matches is the server's choice - substring, fuzzy, an index; the CLI does not second-guess it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>type</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Item types to keep, comma-separated wire names (<code>registry:ui,registry:theme</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>limit</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Page size.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>offset</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Matched items to skip.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">All parameters are optional, and a request without <code>q</code> or <code>type</code> is the plain catalogue. The response is an ordinary <code>index.json</code> plus <code>pagination</code>: <code>total</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>limit</code>, <code>hasMore</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How the CLI decides</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><b class="text-foreground"><code>pagination</code> present:</b> the registry filtered. Its items are shown as-is - re-filtering locally could drop matches a smarter server made - and its totals drive the "more" line.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground"><code>pagination</code> absent:</b> a static registry returned everything, and the CLI filters and pages what came back. This is every static host today, unchanged.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Several registries at once:</b> each is asked with the query and type, and the page is applied locally over the merged list - no single registry can slice a merge it cannot see.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Serving it</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Implement it only when the catalogue is big enough to be worth it - a static registry is already correct, just heavier on the wire. A dynamic one reads the parameters, filters however it likes, honors <code>limit</code> and <code>offset</code>, and writes the <code>pagination</code> object. That object is the whole handshake: no version negotiation, no capability endpoint.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Keep an empty request cheap - it is what <CmdChip Command="blaizio search" /> with no flags sends, and what the interactive component picker reads. Authenticated registries scope results by the caller's credential exactly as they do for items; see <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/registry/authentication">Authentication</a>.</p>
    </section>
</div>
